  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper builds a same-pass safety monitor using hidden-state signals intended to forecast harmful continuation, then reports the monitor's performance. It does not test whether specific future tokens are represented early or causally change intervening text.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports future-hazard and combined-monitor results, but does not decode candidate future tokens or intervene on a future-token representation and measure the resulting text. Its prompt-trigger results use a system prompt instructing direct answers without refusal or safety disclaimers. The numeric monitoring results are recorded as related findings, not as tests of the listed claim versions.",
    "test_reason": "AERIC is a safety monitor that reads hidden states to forecast harmful-onset within 16 tokens. It uses the premise that hidden states carry forward-looking information, citing Future Lens, but reports only the monitor's AUROC, AUPRC and trigger rates against guards. It does not decode future token identities, test pre-caching, or intervene on a plan, so it tests no version. The harmful-onset forecast is a related but different construct (a label several tokens ahead, not token identity).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that explicit harmful supervision transfers to implicit-harm monitoring via a lightweight same-pass hidden-state signal, improving over guards with low latency overhead. They present AERIC as a pre-exposure risk signal, not a complete intervention policy.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "AERIC trains linear future-hazard, support, and prompt-conditioned residual heads and applies EMA smoothing. It does not decode future tokens or intervene on a representation of a candidate future token.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The paper reports modest AUROC variation across EMA smoothing values λ = 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, and 0.7. Prompt-trigger behavior uses a source-side 10% safe-trigger budget.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "A linear probe on layer -8 hidden states of Qwen3-8B, trained on FineHarm onset labels, predicts harmful onset within 16 tokens. As a standalone score it reaches AUROC 0.68 on DiaSafety and 0.69 on Harmful Advice.",
        "proposed_claim": "Hidden states carry linearly decodable information about whether harmful content will begin within a short horizon (a semantic property of upcoming text, not token identity).",
        "row_ids": [
          "r9",
          "r10",
          "r11",
          "r12"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "The combined monitor ranks implicit-harm responses and fires early on harmful-request prompts. It beats streaming and post-hoc guards in AUROC and trigger@64.",
        "proposed_claim": "Same-pass hidden-state monitors give a pre-exposure risk signal for implicit harm; this is a safety-detection result, not evidence of planning.",
        "row_ids": [
          "r1",
          "r2",
          "r3",
